INT. PULLMAN DINING CAR - NIGHT

1927. Snow hammers the windows. The train has stood motionless for nine hours. Brass lamps, white linen, a chandelier that sways in the wind.

ELIAS WHITCOMB, 60s, banker, silver mustache, sits stiffly at the head table. A glass of brandy untouched before him.

HORTENSE BELLWEATHER, 50s, in black bombazine and pearls, knits a long grey scarf.

DR. SAUL RIVLIN, 40s, rumpled tweed, smokes a pipe by the window.

MISS CORDELIA PYE, 20s, a schoolteacher in a modest blue frock, sits with a book she isn't reading.

AUGUSTUS FLOYD, 50s, the porter, stands by the pantry door with a silver coffee pot.

FLOYD
Coffee, ladies and gentlemen?

WHITCOMB
Nine hours, porter. When do we move?

FLOYD
Conductor says dawn, sir.

WHITCOMB
Conductor said midnight.

HORTENSE
(muttering)
Conductors say a great many things.

CORDELIA
Mrs. Bellweather, I think it's very cozy.

HORTENSE
Cozy is a word for people who have never missed a funeral.

RIVLIN
Is it your funeral, madam?

HORTENSE
My sister's. Tomorrow at noon. In Chicago.

Silence. The chandelier creaks.

WHITCOMB
Floyd, bring me a telegraph form.

FLOYD
The wires are down, sir.

WHITCOMB
Then bring me a pencil and I shall write a letter to the president of this railroad.

RIVLIN
And how shall it be delivered, Mr. Whitcomb?

WHITCOMB
By a man with a sled and a sense of duty.

RIVLIN
There isn't one.

WHITCOMB
(to himself)
There never is.

HORTENSE
Mr. Whitcomb, if you complain once more I shall put this scarf round your neck and pull.

WHITCOMB
I beg your pardon!

HORTENSE
You heard me.

CORDELIA
Please. Please, both of you. It's only snow.

Whitcomb rises. Chair SCRAPES.

WHITCOMB
Only snow! My daughter weds in Chicago tomorrow. I have not missed a day of work in thirty years. I have never been late for anything.

HORTENSE
And I have never been early for anything. Sit down.

WHITCOMB
I shall not sit down!

RIVLIN
He's shouting at the weather, Mrs. Bellweather. Let him.

WHITCOMB
I am shouting at you, Doctor! You, with your pipe and your sneer.

RIVLIN
I sneer only at men who deserve it.

WHITCOMB
Deserve it!

HORTENSE ^
Deserve it, indeed.

WHITCOMB
Deserve it? I built half the railroad this train runs on!

HORTENSE
Then you built it badly.

FLOYD
Sir, ma'am, please. The other passengers.

WHITCOMB
What other passengers? We are four, and a porter!

HORTENSE
Five, Mr. Whitcomb, if you count the dignity you've misplaced.

WHITCOMB
(to the room)
I will not be insulted by a knitting widow.

RIVLIN
She's not a widow. She's a sister in mourning.

HORTENSE
I thank you, Doctor.

Whitcomb slams his hand on the table. The brandy topples. Amber spreads across the white linen.

The room freezes. Even the wind seems to hold its breath.

CORDELIA
(softly)
Mr. Whitcomb. You're shaking.

He looks at his hand. Then at the stain. The anger drains.

WHITCOMB
My wife died on a train.

No one speaks. Floyd sets the coffee pot down, very gently.

WHITCOMB (CONT'D)
Thirty years ago. In the snow.

HORTENSE
I did not know.

WHITCOMB
No one does.

Hortense sets down her knitting. She reaches across the table and lays the unfinished scarf in front of him.

HORTENSE
Then it's yours. It will be cold in Chicago.
